{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>May YHWH judge between me and you.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: whoever causes another to tremble before the attribute of justice shall not emerge unscathed from its hand. Sarah deserved to reach the years of Abraham's life, and because she said <b>May YHWH judge</b>, she was deprived of forty-eight (מ\"ח) years of her life. Rabbi Hoshaya said: it is written <b>between me and your son</b>. Now it is already written <b>and he came to Hagar and she conceived</b>, and the embryo was already in her womb — so what does Scripture mean by <b>you are with child and shall bear a son</b>? Rather, it teaches that she cast the evil eye upon her and caused her to miscarry her embryo. Rabbi Hanina said: if even Elisha the prophet had said thus in the holy spirit of HaShem — it would suffice. Thus far in Bereshit Rabbah." "<b>However,</b> since we find <b>וביניך</b> written fully, with two yods, it may be expounded that she turned her face toward Hagar, and to her she said 'May YHWH judge between me and you' — that is, the embryo that is within you; and this is the reason she cast the evil eye upon her."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:42.714299995Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}